1
00:00:05,125 --> 00:00:06,961
[PEOPLE chattering]

2
00:00:07,003 --> 00:00:09,589
[PROSTITUTE 15A]
So, how about right now?

3
00:00:09,630 --> 00:00:11,716
[PROSTITUTE 15A giggles]

4
00:00:12,633 --> 00:00:14,761
[PROSTITUTE 15B]
How much you got?

5
00:00:22,769 --> 00:00:25,688
[POLICEMAN 15A]
Oi, no sleeping down here, miss.

6
00:00:26,856 --> 00:00:27,982
[yelps]

7
00:00:28,024 --> 00:00:33,821
[POLICEMAN 15A gasping]

8
00:00:39,410 --> 00:00:42,538
[WILLIAM] Once again,
the victim was a prostitute.

9
00:00:42,580 --> 00:00:43,957
This is the second murder

10
00:00:43,998 --> 00:00:48,335
to take place in Whitechapel
and there are no suspects, yet.

11
00:00:52,214 --> 00:00:54,592
"Jack the Ripper," they say?

12
00:02:33,774 --> 00:02:38,278
[LOUIS] Please use this room,
Mister Bonde. After you.

13
00:02:38,320 --> 00:02:40,489
[BOTH]
Huh?

14
00:02:40,531 --> 00:02:43,576
Hey! What're you doin'
bargin' in here, you rascal?!

15
00:02:43,617 --> 00:02:45,912
Men only, meaning women
aren't allowed!

16
00:02:45,953 --> 00:02:48,915
Starting this moment,
I'll be using the room as well.

17
00:02:48,956 --> 00:02:49,874
So, cheers, mate.

18
00:02:49,916 --> 00:02:53,419
[gasps] Are you just
gonna stand there?

19
00:02:53,460 --> 00:02:55,922
Well, considering the fact
Mister Bonde is a man,

20
00:02:55,963 --> 00:02:57,006
I see no problem.

21
00:02:57,048 --> 00:02:58,091
[MORAN]
Traitor!

22
00:02:58,132 --> 00:03:00,593
Please, Moran.
We are no different.

23
00:03:00,634 --> 00:03:03,096
And I'd like you
to treat me as such.

24
00:03:03,137 --> 00:03:04,764
This doesn't make me
uncomfortable,

25
00:03:04,805 --> 00:03:06,849
so pay me no mind. Deal?

26
00:03:06,891 --> 00:03:09,852
Just 'cause you're fine with it
doesn't mean I have to be!

27
00:03:09,894 --> 00:03:12,605
Besides,
there're other factors in play.

28
00:03:12,646 --> 00:03:14,107
[BONDE]
Such as?

29
00:03:14,148 --> 00:03:15,441
You may have doubts,

30
00:03:15,482 --> 00:03:18,152
but I'm more than
confident in my abilities.

31
00:03:18,194 --> 00:03:20,113
[MORAN]
I don't just mean appearances.

32
00:03:20,154 --> 00:03:21,363
Face it--

33
00:03:21,405 --> 00:03:23,991
you can't hope to match
a man's upper-body strength.

34
00:03:24,033 --> 00:03:26,827
Look, you simply won't--

35
00:03:28,370 --> 00:03:30,081
You don't know when to quit.

36
00:03:30,123 --> 00:03:31,749
But since it appears
you're dead set

37
00:03:31,791 --> 00:03:35,044
on not acknowledging who I am...

38
00:03:35,086 --> 00:03:37,713
...let's test your
theory... you and I.

39
00:03:37,755 --> 00:03:39,673
[MORAN whimpers]

40
00:03:39,715 --> 00:03:41,550
And what is that
supposed to mean?

41
00:03:41,592 --> 00:03:43,844
Test what? Keep her back!

42
00:03:43,886 --> 00:03:46,764
That's quite enough shouting.
Now, what's wrong here?

43
00:03:46,806 --> 00:03:49,225
Just in time!
Listen to this, William!

44
00:03:49,267 --> 00:03:50,309
Go on, tell him!

45
00:03:50,350 --> 00:03:52,061
Seeing as how I'm a gentleman,

46
00:03:52,103 --> 00:03:55,522
I would like to use this room
with my fellow associates.

47
00:03:55,564 --> 00:03:57,483
What's your take on it, Will?

48
00:03:57,524 --> 00:03:58,859
Based on what I've heard,

49
00:03:58,901 --> 00:04:01,528
you've some issue sharing
this space with your fellow man.

50
00:04:01,570 --> 00:04:02,613
I thought this was serious.

51
00:04:02,655 --> 00:04:05,950
[MORAN gasps]
After all we've been through,

52
00:04:05,992 --> 00:04:08,494
you traitor.

53
00:04:08,535 --> 00:04:09,745
Honestly,

54
00:04:09,787 --> 00:04:13,124
you've got William twisted
around your little finger.

55
00:04:15,375 --> 00:04:16,752
What exactly will it take

56
00:04:16,794 --> 00:04:19,839
for you to finally
acknowledge who I am?

57
00:04:19,880 --> 00:04:21,257
You've got it wrong.

58
00:04:21,299 --> 00:04:24,677
The real question is
whether or not you're useful.

59
00:04:26,804 --> 00:04:28,722
[WILLIAM]
Is everyone present?

60
00:04:28,764 --> 00:04:32,643
I have a task I'd like
you all to work on together.

61
00:04:32,685 --> 00:04:35,395
[MORAN] Together?
So... that includes her?

62
00:04:35,437 --> 00:04:37,190
[WILLIAM]
We need him, yes.

63
00:04:37,231 --> 00:04:40,234
Need I remind you that
you're the senior operative?

64
00:04:40,276 --> 00:04:41,861
Would you show a little support?

65
00:04:41,902 --> 00:04:44,363
Albert did ask you
to take charge of Bonde.

66
00:04:44,404 --> 00:04:47,407
Yeah, fine. I know what he said.

67
00:04:47,449 --> 00:04:50,452
[WILLIAM] The details of the job
are on this piece of paper.

68
00:04:50,494 --> 00:04:54,915
I'd go over them, but at
the moment, I need to rest...

69
00:04:56,834 --> 00:04:58,211
[LOUIS]
Really, Brother.

70
00:04:58,252 --> 00:05:00,587
You're likely to catch a cold
if you sleep there like that.

71
00:05:00,629 --> 00:05:03,132
[BONDE chuckles]
That his normal sleep routine?

72
00:05:03,174 --> 00:05:06,844
It's a sort of side effect
from overexerting his mind.

73
00:05:06,886 --> 00:05:10,514
When he gets this way,
he doesn't wake up for anything.

74
00:05:10,556 --> 00:05:14,727
Now, concerning the
details of our mission...

75
00:05:14,768 --> 00:05:16,395
- -[metal jingles]
- -[MORAN] Hm?

76
00:05:16,436 --> 00:05:18,647
[MORAN]
A key?

77
00:05:22,193 --> 00:05:23,277
[WOMAN 15B]
Ooh!

78
00:05:23,319 --> 00:05:24,904
[WOMAN 15A] Goodness,
look at those gentlemen!

79
00:05:24,945 --> 00:05:27,740
[WOMAN 15B]
My word! How handsome.

80
00:05:27,781 --> 00:05:30,284
[grunts, chuckles]

81
00:05:30,326 --> 00:05:31,410
[WOMAN 15C giggles]

82
00:05:31,451 --> 00:05:33,829
He winked at me!

83
00:05:33,871 --> 00:05:34,747
[MORAN grunts]

84
00:05:34,788 --> 00:05:35,706
[MORAN]
If there was any doubt about

85
00:05:35,748 --> 00:05:37,833
whether Bonde could
pass as one of us,

86
00:05:37,875 --> 00:05:39,960
it's been to put rest.

87
00:05:40,002 --> 00:05:41,379
[MORAN]
I'm not one to complain,

88
00:05:41,420 --> 00:05:45,007
but fetching the contents of a
safe deposit box at the bank?

89
00:05:45,049 --> 00:05:47,260
It's a child's errand;
why ask me?

90
00:05:47,301 --> 00:05:48,886
[LOUIS]
Enough.

91
00:05:48,928 --> 00:05:52,681
William ordered us all to carry
out this task, and we shall.

92
00:05:52,723 --> 00:05:54,058
[MORAN]
All right, fine.

93
00:05:54,100 --> 00:05:57,019
Do you always have
to be so by-the-book?

94
00:05:57,061 --> 00:06:00,814
[BONDE grunts] Well, Fred.
A penny for your thoughts?

95
00:06:02,482 --> 00:06:04,026
[FRED]
This assignment we're on--

96
00:06:04,068 --> 00:06:07,071
William made it a point
for all of us to be involved.

97
00:06:07,113 --> 00:06:11,242
It's out of the ordinary.
That's what's on my mind.

98
00:06:11,284 --> 00:06:13,994
Maybe so. Still...

99
00:06:14,036 --> 00:06:15,955
Regardless of what it entails,

100
00:06:15,996 --> 00:06:19,625
we merely need to carry out
the task assigned to us.

101
00:06:19,666 --> 00:06:21,835
[MORAN]
"The task assigned to us."

102
00:06:21,877 --> 00:06:23,503
Please.

103
00:06:24,880 --> 00:06:27,591
[GEOFF laughs]
Can you believe it?

104
00:06:27,633 --> 00:06:29,385
I was off duty,
but Smith fell ill,

105
00:06:29,427 --> 00:06:31,345
so I was given
his shift instead.

106
00:06:31,387 --> 00:06:32,721
[CUSTOMER 15A]
Is that right?

107
00:06:32,763 --> 00:06:35,266
It's been rough around here
lately, so I'm quite grateful.

108
00:06:35,308 --> 00:06:38,518
Don't worry about a thing!
You have my word, sir!

109
00:06:38,560 --> 00:06:41,730
I'll arrest any criminal
that steps through here!

110
00:06:44,150 --> 00:06:47,069
[LOUIS] I need to access
my safety deposit box.

111
00:06:47,111 --> 00:06:49,405
[BANK TELLER 15A] A pleasure to
see you again, Mister Moriarty.

112
00:06:49,447 --> 00:06:51,115
I'll get things ready at once.

113
00:06:51,157 --> 00:06:53,242
Please wait for me
in the parlor.

114
00:07:01,917 --> 00:07:02,918
What's taking him?

115
00:07:02,960 --> 00:07:04,211
- -[crash]
- -[CUSTOMER 15B screams]

116
00:07:04,253 --> 00:07:05,129
[BANK CUSTOMERS shouting]

117
00:07:05,171 --> 00:07:07,673
[CUSTOMER 15A]
Wh-Who are you people?

118
00:07:07,714 --> 00:07:08,841
[BANK TELLER 15A yelps]

119
00:07:08,882 --> 00:07:11,427
[ROBBER 15A] Stay calm!
No one try to be a hero!

120
00:07:11,469 --> 00:07:12,761
[BANK EMPLOYEES gasp]

121
00:07:12,803 --> 00:07:14,805
[ROBBER 15B] Hand over your
keys and be quick about it!

122
00:07:14,847 --> 00:07:15,764
[CUSTOMERS gasp]

123
00:07:15,806 --> 00:07:16,640
[ROBBER 15C]
Put those hands in the air

124
00:07:16,682 --> 00:07:20,227
and stand in the
corner! Do it now!

125
00:07:27,026 --> 00:07:28,527
[ROBBER 15A]
You two, take the vault.

126
00:07:28,568 --> 00:07:30,112
Loot the safe deposit boxes.

127
00:07:30,154 --> 00:07:32,406
And load up anything
that looks valuable.

128
00:07:32,448 --> 00:07:35,075
[GEOFF] Bollocks,
that must be the leader.

129
00:07:35,117 --> 00:07:37,703
I wasn't expecting a chance
at promotion to come this early.

130
00:07:37,744 --> 00:07:39,579
- -[thud]
- -[GEOFF groans]

131
00:07:39,621 --> 00:07:40,580
[ROBBER 15C]
You wanna live?

132
00:07:40,622 --> 00:07:42,208
Stay down and shut
your mouth, yard rat!

133
00:07:42,249 --> 00:07:43,376
[ROBBER 15A]
On your feet!

134
00:07:43,417 --> 00:07:45,336
You and I are going
to blindfold the customers.

135
00:07:45,378 --> 00:07:46,379
Look lively!

136
00:07:46,420 --> 00:07:49,006
[BONDE]
What luck. A bank robbery.

137
00:07:49,048 --> 00:07:50,883
Why now of all times?

138
00:07:50,924 --> 00:07:53,344
We can't allow the contents
of the safe deposit box

139
00:07:53,386 --> 00:07:54,887
to fall into their hands.

140
00:07:54,928 --> 00:07:57,597
Also, the hostages
are a concern.

141
00:07:57,639 --> 00:07:59,850
If I manage to carry out
my role well enough.

142
00:07:59,892 --> 00:08:02,144
The others will accept
me as one of them.

143
00:08:02,186 --> 00:08:04,271
[gasps]

144
00:08:04,313 --> 00:08:05,689
[BONDE]
Where'd they go?

145
00:08:05,731 --> 00:08:07,441
If they're gone, does
that mean Will's mission

146
00:08:07,483 --> 00:08:09,902
takes priority
over the hostages?

147
00:08:09,943 --> 00:08:11,778
I'm already
getting a late start!

148
00:08:11,820 --> 00:08:14,573
[BONDE panting]

149
00:08:16,700 --> 00:08:18,536
[BONDE]
What?

150
00:08:22,498 --> 00:08:25,042
[BONDE (ADLER)] That certainly
didn't take long.

151
00:08:25,084 --> 00:08:26,293
Where're the others?

152
00:08:26,335 --> 00:08:29,046
[MORAN] Meaning what, Mister
Bonde?

153
00:08:29,088 --> 00:08:32,091
The only reason I'm here
is because I had the key.

154
00:08:32,132 --> 00:08:32,925
- -Huh?

155
00:08:32,966 --> 00:08:34,218
- -To answer your question,
- -Huh?

156
00:08:34,260 --> 00:08:38,097
Louis and Fred are doing
their own jobs, unsupervised.

157
00:08:38,138 --> 00:08:41,725
But when was the
decision made to split up?

158
00:08:41,767 --> 00:08:45,229
[MORAN]
Hm? Just as I suspected.

159
00:08:45,271 --> 00:08:47,147
Earlier, you said
something about

160
00:08:47,189 --> 00:08:49,608
merely doing the assignment
that was given to us,

161
00:08:49,649 --> 00:08:51,527
which gave me pause.

162
00:08:51,569 --> 00:08:54,947
As it turns out you're a person
who takes zero initiative.

163
00:08:54,988 --> 00:08:57,991
[gasps] So what
should I be doing then?

164
00:08:58,033 --> 00:09:00,160
Just running off on my own?

165
00:09:00,202 --> 00:09:02,162
They're two entirely
different things--

166
00:09:02,204 --> 00:09:06,708
going solo versus deciding
yourself what needs to be done.

167
00:09:07,667 --> 00:09:09,169
Well, this is hopeless.

168
00:09:09,211 --> 00:09:12,047
Guess I'll have to play
the role of teacher.

169
00:09:12,089 --> 00:09:16,302
First, name me our top
priority in this situation.

170
00:09:16,343 --> 00:09:18,011
[BONDE grunts]

171
00:09:18,053 --> 00:09:19,472
[BONDE]
We absolutely cannot allow

172
00:09:19,513 --> 00:09:22,099
the item in the safe deposit box
to be taken.

173
00:09:22,141 --> 00:09:23,934
That's our primary objective.

174
00:09:23,976 --> 00:09:25,685
[MORAN]
Correct.

175
00:09:25,727 --> 00:09:27,563
Now that we have it
in our possession,

176
00:09:27,605 --> 00:09:30,816
we could simply leave the
scene and walk out of here.

177
00:09:30,857 --> 00:09:32,151
Should we?

178
00:09:32,192 --> 00:09:34,987
We can't. If we leave
things the way they are,

179
00:09:35,028 --> 00:09:35,988
when the robbers wake up,

180
00:09:36,029 --> 00:09:38,657
they might start executing
the hostages.

181
00:09:38,698 --> 00:09:40,826
[MORAN]
So what do you propose?

182
00:09:40,867 --> 00:09:42,369
We kill them?

183
00:09:42,411 --> 00:09:43,579
Out of the question.

184
00:09:43,621 --> 00:09:45,747
We must avoid all causalities.

185
00:09:45,789 --> 00:09:48,208
Otherwise, the Yard
will be all over the scene.

186
00:09:48,250 --> 00:09:51,211
There's also the risk
Holmes might get involved.

187
00:09:51,253 --> 00:09:53,213
Even if it were
mere coincidence,

188
00:09:53,255 --> 00:09:54,507
we want to avoid the fact

189
00:09:54,548 --> 00:09:57,635
a member of the Moriarty
family was ever present.

190
00:09:57,676 --> 00:10:00,137
We need to bring a swift
end to the robbery--

191
00:10:00,179 --> 00:10:02,264
one which avoids
Holmes's detection.

192
00:10:02,306 --> 00:10:08,895
So why don't you tell us
what we should do, then.

193
00:10:08,937 --> 00:10:10,481
Subdue the robbers
in such a way

194
00:10:10,523 --> 00:10:12,858
that nobody knows we were
the ones responsible.

195
00:10:12,899 --> 00:10:14,901
Then, release the hostages.

196
00:10:14,943 --> 00:10:17,613
[MORAN chuckles]
Right.

197
00:10:17,655 --> 00:10:19,406
Now that we've sorted that out,

198
00:10:19,448 --> 00:10:21,492
you're free to do
whatever you'd like

199
00:10:21,534 --> 00:10:24,370
as long as it doesn't
deviate from the primary goal.

200
00:10:24,411 --> 00:10:28,290
[BONDE gasps]
Whatever I'd like?

201
00:10:28,332 --> 00:10:31,084
The vault room is secure,
so that could be of use.

202
00:10:31,126 --> 00:10:33,462
There are four hostiles
in the main hall.

203
00:10:33,504 --> 00:10:36,256
Speaking of, it's
large and they're spread out.

204
00:10:36,298 --> 00:10:39,301
Taking them down undetected
will be quite a challenge.

205
00:10:39,343 --> 00:10:40,760
He means impossible.

206
00:10:40,802 --> 00:10:44,014
[BONDE chuckles] Not for me.

207
00:10:44,056 --> 00:10:46,058
If I have carte blanche
in this situation,

208
00:10:46,099 --> 00:10:48,686
I'll cash that check.

209
00:10:48,727 --> 00:10:52,189
It may be unorthodox.

210
00:10:52,231 --> 00:10:55,609
But then again,
I never did fit one mould.

211
00:10:55,651 --> 00:10:56,985
[MORAN grunts]

212
00:10:57,027 --> 00:11:01,740
I've an idea how to get out
of this. Care to work with me?

213
00:11:06,662 --> 00:11:10,374
[BANK CUSTOMERS whimper]

214
00:11:12,251 --> 00:11:15,421
[ROBBER 15A] Ugh!
What is taking them so long?

215
00:11:15,462 --> 00:11:17,756
Oi, somebody go see
what's-- [grunts]

216
00:11:17,797 --> 00:11:19,633
[BANK CUSTOMERS gasp]

217
00:11:19,675 --> 00:11:21,093
[ROBBER 15B]
Uh, what's happening?

218
00:11:21,134 --> 00:11:23,762
[ROBBER 15C]
Gunfire? From where?

219
00:11:27,683 --> 00:11:28,934
[BONDE]
One!

220
00:11:28,975 --> 00:11:30,352
Two!

221
00:11:30,394 --> 00:11:31,811
Three!

222
00:11:31,853 --> 00:11:33,313
Four!

223
00:11:33,355 --> 00:11:34,565
Five!

224
00:11:34,607 --> 00:11:36,024
Six!

225
00:11:36,066 --> 00:11:37,610
Seven!

226
00:11:37,651 --> 00:11:38,151
Eight!

227
00:11:38,193 --> 00:11:40,613
[ROBBER 15E groans]

228
00:11:44,157 --> 00:11:46,243
[BONDE] Wake up!
Open your eyes, officer!

229
00:11:46,285 --> 00:11:48,161
[grunts, gasps]

230
00:11:48,203 --> 00:11:50,914
Wow, I've never
seen such heroism!

231
00:11:50,956 --> 00:11:53,792
As expected from one of the
Yard's vaunted officers.

232
00:11:53,833 --> 00:11:55,085
[GEOFF]
Wh--

233
00:11:55,127 --> 00:11:57,879
I'm responsible for that?!

234
00:11:57,921 --> 00:12:00,466
[GEOFF] It's so strange,
I can't recall a thing.

235
00:12:00,507 --> 00:12:02,092
[BONDE]
A heart-stopping feat like that,

236
00:12:02,134 --> 00:12:04,511
it's only natural
you wouldn't remember.

237
00:12:04,553 --> 00:12:07,222
[CUSTOMER 15A] Geoff,
you saved all of our lives.

238
00:12:07,264 --> 00:12:11,435
[BANK CUSTOMERS chatter,
clapping]

239
00:12:11,477 --> 00:12:13,853
[chuckles]

240
00:12:13,895 --> 00:12:16,523
Handling these amateurs
was child's play!

241
00:12:16,565 --> 00:12:18,108
It's like I told you,

242
00:12:18,150 --> 00:12:19,901
any criminal who
steps foot in here

243
00:12:19,943 --> 00:12:22,154
will have to answer to the law!

244
00:12:23,905 --> 00:12:26,575
[MORAN] Quite the ingenious
plan you came up with, Bonde.

245
00:12:26,617 --> 00:12:27,993
Shoot out the lights,

246
00:12:28,034 --> 00:12:30,412
then use the muzzle flashes.

247
00:12:32,665 --> 00:12:34,874
I couldn't have
done it on my own.

248
00:12:34,916 --> 00:12:37,919
Thanks for having
my back. I mean it.

249
00:12:37,961 --> 00:12:40,088
[MORAN chuckles]
Took all four of them out

250
00:12:40,130 --> 00:12:42,841
in a large hall
without a single death.

251
00:12:42,882 --> 00:12:44,968
Then afterwards,
pinned the whole act

252
00:12:45,010 --> 00:12:46,928
on that buffoon of a cop.

253
00:12:46,970 --> 00:12:51,558
I'd say you've
earned passing marks.

254
00:12:51,600 --> 00:12:54,687
We'll have to do
this again, Bonde.

255
00:12:54,728 --> 00:12:57,564
Cheers to our first
successful mission.

256
00:12:57,606 --> 00:12:59,191
Not too much, though.

257
00:12:59,232 --> 00:13:01,026
[BONDE chuckles]

258
00:13:03,612 --> 00:13:05,739
A word, Louis?

259
00:13:05,781 --> 00:13:09,159
Those bank robbers,
I recognized them.

260
00:13:10,118 --> 00:13:13,455
It might've been staged--
the entire thing.

261
00:13:13,497 --> 00:13:16,375
You suspect they were
hired to bring us closer

262
00:13:16,416 --> 00:13:20,128
to Mister Bonde?

263
00:13:20,170 --> 00:13:20,796
Come on!

264
00:13:20,838 --> 00:13:22,255
[LOUIS] Indeed.

265
00:13:22,297 --> 00:13:28,053
Furthermore, I bet I know who
employed them.

266
00:13:28,094 --> 00:13:32,516
[MORAN] You fiend!
Thanks for saving me a drop!

267
00:13:32,558 --> 00:13:35,060
Forget initiative--you
need to learn some manners!

268
00:13:35,101 --> 00:13:38,230
[JACK] What's this? Who's
shouting about manners?

269
00:13:38,271 --> 00:13:40,065
Surely, not Moran.

270
00:13:40,106 --> 00:13:42,317
What? Old man!

271
00:13:42,359 --> 00:13:44,403
Pardon, I mean Master.

272
00:13:44,444 --> 00:13:46,029
Master?

273
00:13:48,323 --> 00:13:52,202
[JACK]
Ahh, this brings back memories.

274
00:13:52,244 --> 00:13:54,496
Who is this gentleman?

275
00:13:56,665 --> 00:14:00,460
That's Jack Renfield.
That's not his only name.

276
00:14:00,502 --> 00:14:03,004
During the First
Anglo-Afghan War,

277
00:14:03,046 --> 00:14:05,965
he was Britain's master
of close-quarters combat--

278
00:14:06,007 --> 00:14:08,260
a man to be feared.

279
00:14:08,301 --> 00:14:10,303
They called him...

280
00:14:10,345 --> 00:14:12,848
...Jack the Ripper.

281
00:14:22,691 --> 00:14:24,443
[MORAN]
Don't be fooled.

282
00:14:24,484 --> 00:14:27,654
This is the actual
"Jack the Ripper."

283
00:14:27,696 --> 00:14:29,030
The real one?

284
00:14:29,072 --> 00:14:32,158
Then you mean the serial killer
is some sort of imposter?

285
00:14:32,200 --> 00:14:33,619
They must be.

286
00:14:33,660 --> 00:14:36,830
True, he went by the nickname
"Jack the Ripper" at one point,

287
00:14:36,872 --> 00:14:39,833
but that was over
half a century ago.

288
00:14:39,875 --> 00:14:41,585
Isn't that right, Master?

289
00:14:41,627 --> 00:14:43,754
[laughs]

290
00:14:43,796 --> 00:14:46,089
I suppose they did call me
that dreadful name

291
00:14:46,131 --> 00:14:47,507
when you were but a lad.

292
00:14:47,549 --> 00:14:50,803
Really? You're going to start
playing the old man card now?

293
00:14:50,844 --> 00:14:52,638
Obviously, William
has called you in

294
00:14:52,679 --> 00:14:54,807
regarding the
Whitechapel murders.

295
00:14:54,848 --> 00:14:56,642
Otherwise, you wouldn't be here.

296
00:14:56,683 --> 00:14:58,393
[WILLIAM]
Sorry.

297
00:14:59,561 --> 00:15:01,229
That's not quite it.

298
00:15:01,271 --> 00:15:04,399
We wake you? You ought
to be getting more rest.

299
00:15:04,441 --> 00:15:07,277
[WILLIAM] No, I need to
inform you all of some details.

300
00:15:07,319 --> 00:15:08,862
[footsteps]

301
00:15:10,906 --> 00:15:13,492
[JACK] It has been far
too long, Master William.

302
00:15:13,533 --> 00:15:16,912
Whatever you request of this
old man in his waning years,

303
00:15:16,954 --> 00:15:18,622
he'll happily oblige.

304
00:15:18,664 --> 00:15:21,541
In fact, I would be
more than grateful.

305
00:15:21,583 --> 00:15:23,418
You needn't be so formal.

306
00:15:23,460 --> 00:15:26,546
Feel free to call me "Young
Will," as you always have.

307
00:15:26,588 --> 00:15:28,214
By the way.

308
00:15:28,256 --> 00:15:29,967
[WILLIAM] Whilst I was sleeping,

309
00:15:30,008 --> 00:15:32,135
you entered the room
and pinched my cheek.

310
00:15:32,177 --> 00:15:33,011
Didn't you?

311
00:15:33,052 --> 00:15:36,306
[laughs] How'd you
know about that?

312
00:15:36,348 --> 00:15:38,350
After all these years,
still the same!

313
00:15:38,391 --> 00:15:39,685
Well, then?

314
00:15:39,726 --> 00:15:42,604
You must've summoned the old
man for an important reason.

315
00:15:42,646 --> 00:15:43,897
[WILLIAM]
Yes.

316
00:15:43,939 --> 00:15:45,440
As you're well aware,

317
00:15:45,482 --> 00:15:47,943
there's a serial killer
loose in Whitechapel.

318
00:15:47,985 --> 00:15:51,363
Claiming credit for the murders
under the maestro's old handle.

319
00:15:51,404 --> 00:15:53,448
In short,

320
00:15:53,490 --> 00:15:56,409
this vile butcher has
besmirched Jack's good name,

321
00:15:56,451 --> 00:15:59,371
and therefore needs
to be dispatched.

322
00:15:59,412 --> 00:16:01,790
Jack sought my assistance
as a crime consultant.

323
00:16:01,832 --> 00:16:03,834
I consented to work with him.

324
00:16:03,876 --> 00:16:05,168
And so,

325
00:16:05,210 --> 00:16:08,463
I sent you to retrieve his
tools of the trade in advance.

326
00:16:08,505 --> 00:16:10,841
Speaking of which, your errand--

327
00:16:10,883 --> 00:16:12,885
I trust it was uneventful?

328
00:16:12,926 --> 00:16:15,512
[BOTH grunt]

329
00:16:15,554 --> 00:16:18,015
[MORAN chuckles] Yeah, sure.
Nothing we couldn't handle.

330
00:16:18,056 --> 00:16:19,558
Agreed, Bonde?

331
00:16:19,599 --> 00:16:21,643
Completely.

332
00:16:23,436 --> 00:16:24,938
I am curious, though.

333
00:16:24,980 --> 00:16:27,065
You refer to him as Maestro.

334
00:16:27,106 --> 00:16:30,068
What sort of relationship
do you all share?

335
00:16:30,109 --> 00:16:33,405
[LOUIS] Ah. We haven't yet told
you that part yet, have we?

336
00:16:33,446 --> 00:16:36,449
After the events of the fire,
the three of us went to stay

337
00:16:36,491 --> 00:16:38,035
at the residence
of Lord Rockwell,

338
00:16:38,076 --> 00:16:40,620
who served as our
guardian for a time.

339
00:16:42,748 --> 00:16:47,502
[LOUIS] And the butler of
that estate was Maestro Jack.

340
00:16:47,544 --> 00:16:48,420
[knocking]

341
00:16:48,461 --> 00:16:50,923
[JACK grunts]
Come in.

342
00:16:54,217 --> 00:16:56,428
[JACK]
Well, who have we here?

343
00:16:56,469 --> 00:16:59,056
The lads from the
House of Moriarty.

344
00:16:59,097 --> 00:17:01,934
Tell me, how can I
be of service to you?

345
00:17:01,975 --> 00:17:03,643
[WILLIAM]
Pardon, Jack Renfield.

346
00:17:03,685 --> 00:17:06,104
But we have an earnest
request to make of you.

347
00:17:06,145 --> 00:17:08,147
Request? Please, name it.

348
00:17:08,189 --> 00:17:10,025
Based on my observations,

349
00:17:10,067 --> 00:17:12,193
it's clear you're former
British military--

350
00:17:12,235 --> 00:17:15,614
a crackerjack soldier who
served in Afghanistan.

351
00:17:15,655 --> 00:17:16,698
[JACK grunts]

352
00:17:16,740 --> 00:17:18,450
[JACK]
How could he know all that?

353
00:17:18,491 --> 00:17:21,661
Surely not through
observation alone.

354
00:17:21,703 --> 00:17:22,746
[WILLIAM]
Additionally,

355
00:17:22,788 --> 00:17:24,873
my research shows
you've earned a moniker--

356
00:17:24,915 --> 00:17:26,374
"Jack the Ripper."

357
00:17:26,416 --> 00:17:27,500
On the battlefield,

358
00:17:27,542 --> 00:17:30,921
you were feared by both
friend and foe alike.

359
00:17:30,963 --> 00:17:34,257
[JACK chuckles] Regarding my
past, I've only let the lord

360
00:17:34,299 --> 00:17:38,762
of the estate in on my
little secret. Tell me.

361
00:17:38,804 --> 00:17:40,221
What is your request?

362
00:17:40,263 --> 00:17:44,142
How may this feeble
old man be of service to you?

363
00:17:44,183 --> 00:17:45,560
It's a brutal world.

364
00:17:45,602 --> 00:17:48,146
If we want to be able to
survive on our own,

365
00:17:48,187 --> 00:17:51,357
we need you to teach us
how to protect ourselves.

366
00:17:51,399 --> 00:17:54,277
And how would I be
able to help with that?

367
00:17:58,364 --> 00:18:00,033
[JACK gasps]

368
00:18:01,660 --> 00:18:05,538
[JACK cackles]
What serious lads!

369
00:18:05,580 --> 00:18:07,540
Very well, I'll teach
you everything

370
00:18:07,582 --> 00:18:09,417
you'll need to
protect yourselves,

371
00:18:09,459 --> 00:18:11,878
or more accurately speaking,

372
00:18:11,920 --> 00:18:15,507
the skills that're
required to kill a man.

373
00:18:15,548 --> 00:18:17,092
[WILLIAM chuckles]

374
00:18:18,969 --> 00:18:21,847
[LOUIS] The maestro taught
us all manner of lessons.

375
00:18:21,888 --> 00:18:24,057
And if it were not
for his tutelage,

376
00:18:24,099 --> 00:18:27,310
this entire organization
would have never existed.

377
00:18:27,352 --> 00:18:29,562
I understand now.

378
00:18:29,604 --> 00:18:32,565
After we reveal the true
identity of the killer,

379
00:18:32,607 --> 00:18:34,192
it will become
clear whether or not

380
00:18:34,233 --> 00:18:36,736
they have any
connection to the maestro.

381
00:18:36,778 --> 00:18:38,530
We will purge society of this

382
00:18:38,571 --> 00:18:40,782
paltry Jack the
Ripper imitation,

383
00:18:40,824 --> 00:18:43,994
ending the scandal and
restoring the maestro's name.

384
00:18:44,036 --> 00:18:45,244
Besides,

385
00:18:45,286 --> 00:18:48,456
it's impossible to overlook
their taste in victims--

386
00:18:48,498 --> 00:18:51,250
how they choose to
prey upon the weak.

387
00:19:03,513 --> 00:19:07,517
Old Whitechapel.
It looks the same, sadly.

388
00:19:07,559 --> 00:19:08,768
[gun fires]

389
00:19:08,810 --> 00:19:09,936
Gunshots?

390
00:19:09,978 --> 00:19:10,854
- -[CITIZEN 15A] Sod off!
- -[CITIZEN 15B] Get out!

391
00:19:10,896 --> 00:19:13,231
The bloody Yard's
not welcome 'ere!

392
00:19:13,272 --> 00:19:16,109
Don't need rats like you
to help keep our streets safe!

393
00:19:16,151 --> 00:19:17,318
[CITIZEN 15D]
You watch!

394
00:19:17,360 --> 00:19:19,654
We're gonna find and
kill that murderer ourselves!

395
00:19:19,696 --> 00:19:21,156
Doesn't make sense
for the residents

396
00:19:21,198 --> 00:19:23,408
to be facing off
against the Yard.

397
00:19:23,449 --> 00:19:26,619
They're after the
same man, right?

398
00:19:26,661 --> 00:19:28,955
It's our job to
protect the citizenry!

399
00:19:28,997 --> 00:19:30,916
We'll handle
the killer, not you!

400
00:19:30,957 --> 00:19:32,709
Well, when do you
plan on startin'?!

401
00:19:32,751 --> 00:19:35,420
You lot haven't investigated
anything so far!

402
00:19:35,461 --> 00:19:38,464
As if you've ever thought
of us as "the citizenry"!

403
00:19:38,506 --> 00:19:39,507
[grunts]

404
00:19:39,549 --> 00:19:41,760
[POLICEMEN gasp]

405
00:19:41,801 --> 00:19:45,430
This whole district's a
powder keg about to explode.

406
00:19:45,471 --> 00:19:48,474
You're right; one false
step is all it will take.

407
00:19:48,516 --> 00:19:50,476
And then the match
will be struck.

408
00:19:50,518 --> 00:19:54,689
This killer has put the
entire town on the precipice.

409
00:19:54,731 --> 00:19:58,610
The battle for Whitechapel
is about to begin.

410
00:19:58,651 --> 00:20:00,946
[ARTERTON grunts]

411
00:20:00,987 --> 00:20:03,907
Those pests think
they run the show?!

412
00:20:03,949 --> 00:20:05,075
Listen, men.

413
00:20:05,117 --> 00:20:07,160
Starting now, Scotland Yard's
immediate priority

414
00:20:07,202 --> 00:20:09,495
is the disarmament of
the Vigilance Committee!

415
00:20:09,537 --> 00:20:11,456
[LESTRADE]
Chief Inspector Arterton!

416
00:20:11,497 --> 00:20:12,874
I believe the safety of the

417
00:20:12,916 --> 00:20:15,210
citizens ought to
come before anything else!

418
00:20:15,252 --> 00:20:17,963
We should focus all our efforts
on arresting the murderer!

419
00:20:18,004 --> 00:20:19,714
[footsteps]

420
00:20:19,756 --> 00:20:21,091
[ARTERTON]
Is that so, Lestrade?

421
00:20:21,133 --> 00:20:24,177
Listen, you clearly don't
understand the nature of things.

422
00:20:24,219 --> 00:20:26,054
It makes no difference
how many dregs

423
00:20:26,096 --> 00:20:29,641
that maniac carves up;
let him kill them all!

424
00:20:29,682 --> 00:20:32,144
But the second those
vigilantes stage an uprising,

425
00:20:32,185 --> 00:20:34,604
it will be too late!

426
00:20:34,646 --> 00:20:37,274
Or have you forgotten
about Bloody Sunday,

427
00:20:37,315 --> 00:20:40,152
when the paupers
clashed with us?!

428
00:20:40,193 --> 00:20:44,072
A blight on our record and
mistake that can't be repeated.

429
00:20:44,114 --> 00:20:46,866
Seriously, who cares
how many whores get killed?!

430
00:20:46,908 --> 00:20:49,661
Disarm the Vigilance
Committee like I ordered!

431
00:20:49,702 --> 00:20:51,579
[INSPECTORS]
Sir!

432
00:20:51,621 --> 00:20:53,290
[LESTRADE grunts]

433
00:20:54,916 --> 00:20:56,293
Quite the show.

434
00:20:56,334 --> 00:21:00,505
I've never seen Chief Inspector
Arterton in such an uproar.

435
00:21:00,546 --> 00:21:03,716
Seems the commissioner--
no, even the interior minister

436
00:21:03,758 --> 00:21:06,427
must be putting a lot
of pressure on him.

437
00:21:06,469 --> 00:21:09,764
Paterson, I'm stepping
out for an hour or so.

438
00:21:09,806 --> 00:21:12,934
Handle any emergencies
that come up, would you?

439
00:21:12,976 --> 00:21:17,105
As if you need to ask.
We're chums, aren't we?

440
00:21:23,862 --> 00:21:25,530
Interesting.

441
00:21:25,571 --> 00:21:27,615
What's that you're
all looking at?

442
00:21:27,657 --> 00:21:30,493
[WILLIAM] Investigative reports
from our man inside the Yard.

443
00:21:30,535 --> 00:21:32,162
[BONDE (ADLER)] An inside man?

444
00:21:32,204 --> 00:21:33,621
Indeed, and the two of you

445
00:21:33,663 --> 00:21:36,333
will most likely be
working together soon.

446
00:21:36,374 --> 00:21:38,543
Really? I can't wait.

447
00:21:38,584 --> 00:21:39,836
Based on these reports,

448
00:21:39,878 --> 00:21:43,422
Scotland Yard's not even
close to arresting a suspect.

449
00:21:43,464 --> 00:21:46,176
[WILLIAM] Yes. They seem
fixated on the theory

450
00:21:46,218 --> 00:21:48,970
this is work of a single
psychotic killer,

451
00:21:49,012 --> 00:21:53,058
but I'm able to more clearly see
the full scope of these murders.

452
00:21:53,099 --> 00:21:54,392
[GROUP grunts]

453
00:21:54,433 --> 00:21:55,352
[WILLIAM]
We're going to teach

454
00:21:55,393 --> 00:21:58,230
these phony Jack
the Rippers a lesson.

455
00:21:58,271 --> 00:22:02,901
Soon they'll discover who the
real devils around here are.

456
00:23:34,742 --> 00:23:37,578
[JACK] Next time on
Moriarty the Patriot  :

457
00:23:37,620 --> 00:23:40,415
"The Phantom of
Whitechapel Act Two."
